"A packet format diagram assigns protocol fields to explicit bit or byte positions in a serialized message.\n\nUse when: Explain a binary protocol header or parser boundary. Review field lengths, alignment, and variable-length sections.\n\nAvoid when: The objective is network topology or message exchange order.\n\nVisual grammar: A fixed-width grid encodes bit or byte offsets. Cells show field names and widths. Continuation rows or brackets identify variable-length payloads and options.\n\nBuild: Name the protocol and exact version or profile. Choose a row width and label bit or byte numbering. Place fixed fields at their specified offsets. Show variable regions separately with clear length rules and enough space for wrapped field names.\n\nCheck: Field widths sum to the specified fixed-header length. Byte order and offset numbering are explicit. Optional fields and padding follow the actual protocol. Grid lines never pass through field text or length annotations.\n\nPitfalls: Drawing proportional widths without stating whether they encode actual field sizes. Using an invented sample value as a protocol requirement.\n\nExample: An IPv4 header layout shows version, header length, total length, addresses, and the optional-header region at specified bit positions.\n\nAlternatives: DNS resolution diagram, Sequence diagram."
